## Step 1 — Run the server

You need Node.js installed on a computer or a cheap server (a $5/month VPS
works fine, or even a PC at your shop that stays on).

```bash
cd server
cp .env.example .env
# open .env and set ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_PASSWORD, JWT_SECRET, SHOP_NAME, SHOP_PHONE
npm install
npm start
```

This alone gets you the dashboard at `http://localhost:3000` — but locking
won't work yet until Firebase is connected (next step).

If you want the dashboard reachable from outside your shop's wifi (e.g. from
your phone while you're out), host it on a small VPS (DigitalOcean, a local
Pakistani VPS provider, etc.) and put a free HTTPS certificate in front of it
with something like Caddy or Nginx + Let's Encrypt. Keep the admin password
strong — anyone with it can lock/unlock any paired phone.

## Step 2 — Create a Firebase project (free)

This is what lets the server "push" the lock command to a phone instantly,
anywhere it has internet.

1. Go to https://console.firebase.google.com → **Add project** (the free
   Spark plan is enough).
2. Inside the project, click the Android icon to **add an Android app**.
   - Package name: `com.nadeemmobile.lock` (must match exactly)
3. Download the `google-services.json` it gives you.
   Put it at `android-app/app/google-services.json`.
4. In the Firebase console, go to **Project settings → Service accounts →
   Generate new private key**. This downloads a different JSON file — this
   one is for the *server*, not the phone.
   Put it at `server/firebase-service-account.json`.
5. Restart the server (`npm start`). If it's working, you won't see the
   "[firebase] Could not load service account" warning in the terminal.

## Step 3 (recommended) — Set your shop's Google account for extra protection

Open `android-app/app/src/main/java/com/nadeemmobile/lock/AppConfig.kt` and
put the Gmail address you'll use to set up phones:

```kotlin
const val SHOP_GOOGLE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL = "yourshop@gmail.com"
```

This makes a hardware-level factory reset (done outside Android, via
recovery mode) still ask for that Google account's password before the phone
can be used again.

## Step 4 — Build the Android app

You'll need **Android Studio** (free, from developer.android.com) on a
Windows/Mac/Linux computer:

1. Open Android Studio → **Open** → select the `android-app` folder.
2. Let it download dependencies (first time takes a few minutes, needs internet).
3. Build → Build APK, or just press the green Run ▶ button with a phone
   connected. This installs the app for testing.

## Step 5 — Set up each phone at the time of sale

This is the one part that needs a bit of care, but it's a 5-minute routine
once you've done it a couple of times.

1. **Factory reset the phone** (Settings → System → Reset, or it's simply new/unused).
2. On the very first "Welcome" setup screen, **before adding any Google
   account**, turn on Developer Options and USB debugging:
   - Tap the welcome screen logo differently per phone brand, or on the
     "Copy apps & data" screen skip it, get to the home screen if it lets you
     without an account, go to Settings → About phone → tap "Build number" 7
     times → Developer options → enable USB debugging.
   - (If the phone forces a Google account before you can reach Settings,
     use the QR-code provisioning method instead — search "Android QR code
     device owner provisioning" for the alternative flow. The ADB method
     below is simpler when the phone allows it.)
3. Connect the phone to your PC by USB, and install the app + make it device owner:
   ```bash
   adb install app-release.apk
   adb shell dpm set-device-owner com.nadeemmobile.lock/.admin.LockDeviceAdminReceiver
   ```
   This command only works if **no Google account is signed in yet** and no
   other device admin app is active — that's why order matters.

   Worth knowing: Google's own documentation calls this ADB method a
   "development and testing tool," not their official production method —
   their supported production route is QR-code provisioning during setup
   (See "Option B" below for that route — I've built it into this project.)
   In practice, plenty of small shops use the ADB method
   exactly like this because it's simpler and it does work — but test it on
   one phone of each brand you sell before rolling it out to many, since a
   few manufacturers (some Samsung/OnePlus models, for example) behave
   slightly differently.
4. Open the **Nadeem Mobile Lock** app on the phone.
5. On your dashboard (in a browser), click **Add customer**, fill in their
   details, and you'll get a 6-digit pairing code.
6. Type the server address and that code into the app on the phone, tap
   **Protect this phone**. You should see "Protected."
7. Now let the customer finish normal phone setup (add their Google account,
   etc.) — the protections stay in place regardless.
8. Hand over the phone.

### Option B — QR code provisioning (no PC/cable needed per phone)

This is Google's official production method, and it scales better if you're
setting up several phones a day: print or display one QR code, and any staff
member can scan it on a factory-reset phone in about 30 seconds — no laptop,
no USB cable, no ADB commands.

**One-time setup:**

1. Build a signed release APK in Android Studio (Build → Generate Signed App Bundle / APK).
2. Host that APK file somewhere with a real **HTTPS** address — the easiest
   way is to drop it into `server/public/nadeem-mobile-lock.apk`, since your
   server already serves that folder as static files. Once your server has
   HTTPS set up (see Step 1 above), it'll be reachable at
   `https://yourdomain.com/nadeem-mobile-lock.apk`.
   (Plain http:// will not work here — Android's setup wizard refuses to
   download the APK over an insecure connection during provisioning.)
3. Compute the APK's checksum:
   ```bash
   cd server/tools
   ./compute-checksum.sh /path/to/app-release.apk
   # Windows: .\compute-checksum.ps1 C:\path\to\app-release.apk
   ```
4. Generate the QR code:
   ```bash
   cd server
   npm install
   npm run generate-qr -- --checksum "PASTE_CHECKSUM_HERE" --url "https://yourdomain.com/nadeem-mobile-lock.apk"
   ```
   Add `--ssid "Your Wifi Name" --password "your-wifi-password"` if you want
   the phone to auto-join your shop's wifi during setup.
5. This saves `server/tools/provisioning-qr.png`. Print it, or display it
   full-screen on a second phone or a monitor at the counter.

**Per phone, from then on:**

1. Factory reset (or use a new, unused phone).
2. On the Welcome screen, tap the same spot on the screen 6 times.
3. Connect to wifi if it asks (skip if you baked wifi into the QR).
4. Scan the QR code.
5. The phone downloads the app and becomes device owner automatically —
   then just open the app to pair it (steps 4–8 above).

If you rebuild the app with different signing, you'll need to regenerate
the QR (the checksum changes). Rebuilding with the *same* signing key keeps
the old QR working.

From here on, it behaves like a completely normal phone for the customer —
nothing is locked or restricted in daily use — until you tap **Lock** on
their name in your dashboard.

